            <ArticleTitle>Giant thrombus trapped in foramen ovale with pulmonary embolus and stroke.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>916-8</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>We describe the case of a young man who, while he was in coma because of a traffic accident, had first a pulmonary embolus and immediately afterwards had a systemic (cerebral) embolus. A transesophageal echocardiographic image revealed a giant thrombi trapped in foramen ovale protruding in right and left ventricles, diagnosing, thus, a paradoxical embolism. The relationship between patent foramen ovale and pulmonary embolism has been reported in some series. Elevated right-chamber pressure caused by pulmonary hypertension could favor the establishment of a right-to-left shunt, causing, in some cases, paradoxical embolisms. We review the clinical implications.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
